## COMMON MISTAKES TO AVOID

## VERIFICATION CHECKLIST

[chk-00003] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: Cross-reference the final sample selection against the full population list to verify that every unique combination of required categories (e.g., Division/Sub-Division/Country) is represented.
[chk-00005] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: Before generating the final output, perform a 'structural audit': verify that all table cells containing lists use bullet points, all citations follow the requested style (e.g., APA: (Author, Year)), and the response begins immediately with the requested content (no preamble).
[chk-00010] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: [chk-00006] :: When a rubric requires metadata for instruments, include an Appendix table with the columns: [Instrument Name] | [Intended Respondent] | [Data Type (Quant/Qual)] | [Source/Adaptation Status]. For ethical requirements, include a dedicated 'Confidentiality and Ethical Safeguards' section explicitly citing relevant standards (e.g., HIPAA, IRB).
## GENERAL PRINCIPLES

[prin-00009] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: [gp-00001] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: Adopt a 'compliance-first' drafting approach: treat the rubric as a mandatory checklist. When a specific length is requested, ensure the depth of detail (e.g., defining every cost driver and approval gate) is sufficient to naturally meet the length requirement, avoiding high-level summaries.
[prin-00011] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: [prin-00010] :: Treat evaluation rubrics as a rigid 'schema' rather than a suggestion. Before finalizing, map every individual rubric criterion to a specific section or sentence in the draft. If a requirement asks for 'accommodations' or 'safeguards,' these must be explicitly named as distinct, actionable policies rather than implied through general professional tone.
## PROBLEM-SOLVING HEURISTICS

[ph-00002] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: For tasks requiring multi-dimensional categorical coverage, construct a 'coverage matrix' or checklist before selection. Map every required category (e.g., Division, Country) to at least one data point in the selection set to ensure full compliance.
[ph-00008] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: [ph-00003] helpful=0 harmful=0 :: For multi-page procedural documents, create a 'Requirement-to-Section' matrix before drafting. Map every rubric constraint (stakeholders, process steps, cost drivers) to a specific section header to ensure 100% coverage of mandatory entities.
